///! Program entry — both tiers.
///!
///! This module owns the entry. `main` is an external C-ABI symbol resolved from
///! the user object at link time; the entry calls it and terminates with its
///! return value through `CryoRt::Sys::rt_exit`.
///!
///! One link does not use it: a fully static glibc (`--release-static`) sets its
///! own thread control block up inside `__libc_start_main`, which is the job
///! ld.so does before the entry runs in a dynamic link, so entering past it
///! leaves TLS unmapped. That link keeps crt0's entry; the driver decides, and
///! nothing here changes.
///!
///! The x86-64 ISA is shared by AMD and Intel CPUs, so one x86-64 codepath
///! covers both vendors; the split that matters is OS ABI. The entry stub and
///! argument capture are per-OS (gated `![config(linux)]` / `![config(windows)]`)
///! because the two OSes hand arguments over by different mechanisms:
///!
///!   - Linux: the kernel enters with `%rsp` at argc. The stub is `![naked]` so
///!     `%rsp` still points at the initial process stack when its body runs, and
///!     stages argc/argv into the SysV arg registers.
///!   - Windows: PE startup passes no stack arguments at all — the loader
///!     `call`s the entry like an ordinary function. Arguments come from the PEB
///!     instead: `ProcessParameters.CommandLine`, split per the CRT's quoting
///!     rules and transcoded UTF-16 -> UTF-8 (see `capture_os_args`).
///!
///! The two tiers differ in what stands between the OS and `main`:
///!
///!   - FREESTANDING (`no_runtime`): the link passes `-nostartfiles -nostdlib`,
///!     so nothing else exists. `_start` is the entry, `main` keeps its declared
///!     zero-arg signature (not widened to argc/argv/envp), and exit is a raw
///!     kernel call. See `__cryo_start`.
///!   - HOSTED: libc and the crt objects ARE linked — only their `main`-calling
///!     startup is bypassed, by pointing the linker's entry at `__cryo_entry`.
///!     `main` carries the widened C signature and the entry owns the pre-`main`
///!     initialisation crt0 would otherwise have done. See `__cryo_hosted_start`.
///!
///! A hosted link deliberately does NOT pass `-nostartfiles`. Dropping the crt
///! objects removes far more than an entry point: on Linux it takes
///! `__dso_handle` (crtbegin.o) and `_init` (crti.o), on Windows `atexit` and
///! `_pei386_runtime_relocator` (crt2.o) — and `atexit` is referenced
///! unconditionally by `libmingwex.a`, so it cannot be dodged by not calling it.
///! Every one of those is a LINK failure, and re-providing them from here would
///! mean reimplementing libc's exit machinery. Retaining the crt objects and
///! overriding only the entry symbol keeps them all, at the cost of some unused
///! startup code in the image.

namespace CryoRt::Core::Entry;

import CryoRt::Sys;

/// The user program's C-ABI entry, resolved by the linker from the user object.
///
/// The signature follows what codegen emitted, which differs by tier: a
/// freestanding `main` keeps the zero-arg form the user wrote, while a hosted
/// one is widened to the C ABI's `int main(int, char**, char**)` (and carries a
/// prologue publishing argv/envp to the stdlib). Declaring the wrong arity here
/// would not fail to link — `main` is C-ABI — it would hand `main` uninitialised
/// argument registers.
![config(no_runtime)]
extern "C" {
    function main() -> i32;
}

![config(not(no_runtime))]
extern "C" {
    function main(argc: i32, argv: i8**, envp: i8**) -> i32;
}

// --- Linux ------------------------------------------------------------------

/// ELF entry. Naked: no prologue/epilogue, so `%rsp` still points at the
/// initial process stack when the body runs — argc at `(%rsp)`, argv at
/// `8(%rsp)`, staged into rdi/rsi for `__cryo_start`. Clears `%rbp` to
/// terminate stack-trace unwinding at the entry, realigns `%rsp` to 16 for the
/// SysV call boundary, then tail-calls into Cryo.
![naked] ![no_mangle] ![config(no_runtime)] ![config(linux)]
function _start() {
    ![arch(x86_64, att)]
    asm {
        xorq %rbp, %rbp
        movq (%rsp), %rdi
        leaq 8(%rsp), %rsi
        andq $-16, %rsp
        call __cryo_start
    }
}

/// ELF entry for a HOSTED link. Naked for the same reason `_start` is: the
/// kernel enters with `%rsp` at argc rather than on a call frame, so the body
/// must run before any prologue disturbs it.
///
/// Named `__cryo_entry` rather than `_start` because a hosted link retains
/// crt1.o, which already defines `_start`; the linker is aimed here with
/// `-Wl,-e,__cryo_entry`, which also pulls this member out of the tier archive.
![naked] ![no_mangle] ![config(not(no_runtime))] ![config(linux)]
function __cryo_entry() {
    ![arch(x86_64, att)]
    asm {
        xorq %rbp, %rbp
        movq (%rsp), %rdi
        leaq 8(%rsp), %rsi
        andq $-16, %rsp
        call __cryo_hosted_start
    }
}

/// The legacy DT_INIT hook, from crti.o. glibc's `call_init` runs it between the
/// two initializer arrays, so this tier does too.
![config(not(no_runtime))] ![config(linux)]
extern "C" {
    function _init() -> void;
}

/// One entry of an ELF initializer array: a pointer-sized slot holding an
/// initializer that takes the process arguments.
///
/// The slot is described as a struct rather than read as a raw integer and cast,
/// because `as` accepts no function type — casting to `(i32, i8**, i8**) -> void`
/// does not parse. Loading the field gives the same call through a typed handle.
![config(not(no_runtime))] ![config(linux)]
type struct InitSlot {
    run: (i32, i8**, i8**) -> void;
}

/// Bounds of the two initializer arrays.
///
/// These four symbols have no declaration to import: ld synthesises them around
/// the `.preinit_array` / `.init_array` output sections, and they exist only as
/// addresses — there is no object behind them to give a Cryo type. A
/// RIP-relative LEA is how you name such a symbol without declaring it. Each is
/// `![weak]`-equivalent by construction: ld defines start == end when the
/// section is absent, so an empty array walks zero entries rather than failing
/// to link.
![config(not(no_runtime))] ![config(linux)]
function preinit_array_start() -> u64 {
    mut a: u64 = 0;
    ![arch(x86_64, att)]
    asm {
        leaq __preinit_array_start(%rip), %rax
        movq %rax, ${=a:"rax"}
    }
    return a;
}

![config(not(no_runtime))] ![config(linux)]
function preinit_array_end() -> u64 {
    mut a: u64 = 0;
    ![arch(x86_64, att)]
    asm {
        leaq __preinit_array_end(%rip), %rax
        movq %rax, ${=a:"rax"}
    }
    return a;
}

![config(not(no_runtime))] ![config(linux)]
function init_array_start() -> u64 {
    mut a: u64 = 0;
    ![arch(x86_64, att)]
    asm {
        leaq __init_array_start(%rip), %rax
        movq %rax, ${=a:"rax"}
    }
    return a;
}

![config(not(no_runtime))] ![config(linux)]
function init_array_end() -> u64 {
    mut a: u64 = 0;
    ![arch(x86_64, att)]
    asm {
        leaq __init_array_end(%rip), %rax
        movq %rax, ${=a:"rax"}
    }
    return a;
}

/// Run every initializer in `[lo, hi)`, in order, each receiving the process
/// arguments. Entries are pointer-sized, hence the 8-byte stride.
![config(not(no_runtime))] ![config(linux)]
function run_init_range(lo: u64, hi: u64, argc: i32, argv: i8**, envp: i8**) -> void {
    mut p: u64 = lo;
    while (p < hi) {
        const slot: InitSlot* = p as InitSlot*;
        const init: (i32, i8**, i8**) -> void = slot.run;
        init(argc, argv, envp);
        p = p + 8;
    }
}

// --- Windows ----------------------------------------------------------------

/// PE entry. Naked: the Windows loader passes no stack arguments, so the body
/// only establishes a valid call frame — clears `%rbp`, aligns `%rsp` to 16,
/// and reserves the 32-byte register home ("shadow") space every Win64 callee
/// requires — before tail-calling into Cryo. rcx/rdx (the Win64 first two
/// integer arg registers) are zeroed because there is nothing to stage into
/// them: `__cryo_start`'s parameters are unused on this OS, and the real
/// arguments are read from the PEB by `capture_os_args`.
![naked] ![no_mangle] ![config(no_runtime)] ![config(windows)]
function _start() {
    ![arch(x86_64, att)]
    asm {
        xorq %rbp, %rbp
        xorq %rcx, %rcx
        xorq %rdx, %rdx
        andq $-16, %rsp
        subq $32, %rsp
        call __cryo_start
    }
}

/// The mingw backend emits `call __main` in `main`'s prologue to run C global
/// constructors. A freestanding build has no CRT to supply it; this empty
/// definition satisfies that call. When the runtime gains constructor support
/// (an `.init_array` / `global_ctors` emitter), the registered constructors
/// run from here.
![no_mangle] ![config(no_runtime)] ![config(windows)]
function __main() {
}

/// PE entry for a HOSTED link. Not `![naked]`: the Windows loader `call`s the
/// entry on an ordinary, aligned call frame, so a normal function body is a
/// valid entry — and a real frame carries the `.pdata` unwind info a naked stub
/// would not.
///
/// Its parameters are the ones `__cryo_hosted_start` ignores on this OS; zero is
/// passed because the arguments come from the PEB (see `capture_os_args`).
![no_mangle] ![config(not(no_runtime))] ![config(windows)]
function __cryo_entry() -> never {
    __cryo_hosted_start(0, null);
}

/// Startup services the retained crt objects provide, which a retargeted entry
/// must invoke itself because mingw's own startup — the code that normally calls
/// them — is exactly what was bypassed.
///
///   - `_pei386_runtime_relocator` applies the pseudo-relocations backing
///     auto-imported DLL *data*, which the user's program may well have even
///     though this tier no longer does.
///   - `__main` runs the C global constructors. It is idempotent (it guards on a
///     static flag), so calling it here is safe alongside the `call __main` the
///     mingw backend emits into `main`'s own prologue.
///
/// Nothing here reaches for the CRT's environment block. `__p__environ` exists
/// only in UCRT and the MSVC-runtime import libraries; `_environ` only in
/// msvcrt. Which of the two a given mingw-w64 ships varies by build — binding to
/// either one links on one toolchain and fails on the next, which is precisely
/// how the cross-compile broke while the native build stayed green. The
/// environment comes from the PEB instead (see `capture_os_envp`), the same
/// place argv already comes from.
![config(not(no_runtime))] ![config(windows)]
extern "C" {
    function _pei386_runtime_relocator() -> void;
    function __main() -> void;
}

/// ntdll process-heap allocation, used to size the argv vector to the actual
/// command line instead of a fixed cap. The Windows loader creates the process
/// heap before the PE entry runs, so this is available to the entry itself —
/// no allocator tier required, and the tier's dependency contract stays at
/// kernel32 + ntdll (notably: no shell32, which `CommandLineToArgvW` needs).
![config(windows)]
extern "C" {
    function RtlAllocateHeap(heap: u64, flags: u32, size: u64) -> u8*;
}

/// The PEB, read from the TEB's `ProcessEnvironmentBlock` slot. The TEB is
/// addressed through `%gs` on x86-64, so the PEB pointer is a single load from
/// `%gs:0x60` — no import and no syscall, which is what makes it usable this
/// early.
![config(windows)]
function current_peb() -> u64 {
    mut peb: u64 = 0;
    ![arch(x86_64, att)]
    asm {
        movq %gs:0x60, %rax
        movq %rax, ${=peb:"rax"}
    }
    return peb;
}

/// Read the u64 stored at `addr`.
![config(windows)]
function load64(addr: u64) -> u64 {
    const p: u64* = addr as u64*;
    return p[0];
}

/// Read the u16 stored at `addr`, widened. Used for both `UNICODE_STRING`
/// byte lengths and individual UTF-16 code units.
![config(windows)]
function load16(addr: u64) -> u32 {
    const p: u16* = addr as u16*;
    return p[0] as u32;
}

/// Append `cp` to `buf` at `*pos` as UTF-8, advancing `*pos`. A code point
/// needs at most 4 bytes, and reaching the 4-byte form costs two UTF-16 units,
/// so the output never exceeds 3 bytes per input unit — the bound the caller's
/// buffer is sized against.
![config(windows)]
function append_utf8(buf: u8*, pos: u64*, cp: u32) -> void {
    if (cp < 128) {
        buf[*pos] = cp as u8;                            *pos = *pos + 1;
        return;
    }
    if (cp < 2048) {
        buf[*pos] = (192 + (cp >> 6)) as u8;             *pos = *pos + 1;
        buf[*pos] = (128 + (cp & 63)) as u8;             *pos = *pos + 1;
        return;
    }
    if (cp < 65536) {
        buf[*pos] = (224 + (cp >> 12)) as u8;            *pos = *pos + 1;
        buf[*pos] = (128 + ((cp >> 6) & 63)) as u8;      *pos = *pos + 1;
        buf[*pos] = (128 + (cp & 63)) as u8;             *pos = *pos + 1;
        return;
    }
    buf[*pos] = (240 + (cp >> 18)) as u8;                *pos = *pos + 1;
    buf[*pos] = (128 + ((cp >> 12) & 63)) as u8;         *pos = *pos + 1;
    buf[*pos] = (128 + ((cp >> 6) & 63)) as u8;          *pos = *pos + 1;
    buf[*pos] = (128 + (cp & 63)) as u8;                 *pos = *pos + 1;
}

/// Transcode the UTF-16 code unit at `*i` into `buf` and advance `*i` past the
/// unit(s) consumed. A high surrogate immediately followed by a low surrogate
/// is combined into the one code point it encodes; an unpaired surrogate is
/// passed through as-is rather than dropped, so a malformed command line still
/// round-trips its byte count instead of silently losing an argument.
![config(windows)]
function emit_unit(cmd: u64, n: u64, i: u64*, buf: u8*, pos: u64*) -> void {
    const hi: u32 = load16(cmd + (*i) * 2);
    *i = *i + 1;
    if (hi >= 0xD800 && hi < 0xDC00 && *i < n) {
        const lo: u32 = load16(cmd + (*i) * 2);
        if (lo >= 0xDC00 && lo < 0xE000) {
            *i = *i + 1;
            append_utf8(buf, pos, 0x10000 + ((hi - 0xD800) << 10) + (lo - 0xDC00));
            return;
        }
    }
    append_utf8(buf, pos, hi);
}

/// Capture arguments from the PEB's `ProcessParameters.CommandLine`.
///
/// The command line arrives as one UTF-16 `UNICODE_STRING`, so this owns both
/// the split and the encoding conversion. The split follows the CRT's documented
/// quoting rules, which `CommandLineToArgvW` also implements:
///
///   - `2n` backslashes before a quote produce `n` backslashes and the quote
///     acts as a delimiter; `2n+1` produce `n` backslashes and a literal quote.
///   - Backslashes not followed by a quote are literal (they are path
///     separators on this OS, not escapes).
///   - Inside quotes, `""` is a literal quote and whitespace is ordinary text.
///   - `argv[0]` uses the simpler rule the CRT applies to it: a leading quote
///     runs to the next quote, otherwise the token ends at the first
///     whitespace, and backslash is never an escape.
///
/// Both buffers are sized from the actual command-line length and allocated
/// once, so no valid command line can overflow them and nothing is silently
/// truncated: the text needs at most 3 bytes per unit plus one NUL per
/// argument, and every argument after `argv[0]` costs at least a character and
/// a separator, bounding the count at `n/2 + 3`.
///
/// A failure to reach the PEB, or a heap allocation failure, leaves argc 0 /
/// argv null — the same "unavailable" state callers already handle — rather
/// than a partially built vector.
///
/// Shared by both tiers, not just the freestanding one. A hosted build could
/// have taken argv from the CRT instead, but `__getmainargs` yields codepage-
/// mangled ANSI text; this parser yields UTF-8, which is what Cryo wants, and
/// keeping one implementation means one set of quoting rules to be right about.
![config(windows)]
function capture_os_args(argc: i64, argv: i8**) -> void {
    const peb: u64 = current_peb();
    if (peb == 0) { return; }
    const params: u64 = load64(peb + 0x20);
    if (params == 0) { return; }
    const cmd: u64 = load64(params + 0x78);
    const n: u64 = (load16(params + 0x70) / 2) as u64;
    if (cmd == 0 || n == 0) { return; }

    const heap: u64 = load64(peb + 0x30);
    if (heap == 0) { return; }
    const slots_cap: u64 = n / 2 + 3;
    const buf: u8* = RtlAllocateHeap(heap, 0, n * 3 + slots_cap + 1);
    if (buf == null) { return; }
    const slots: u8* = RtlAllocateHeap(heap, 0, slots_cap * 8);
    if (slots == null) { return; }
    const vec: i8** = slots as i8**;

    mut pos: u64 = 0;
    mut i: u64 = 0;
    mut count: i64 = 0;

    // argv[0].
    vec[0] = ((buf as u64) + pos) as i8*;
    if (load16(cmd) == 34) {
        i = 1;
        while (i < n && load16(cmd + i * 2) != 34) {
            emit_unit(cmd, n, &i, buf, &pos);
        }
        if (i < n) { i = i + 1; }
    } else {
        while (i < n) {
            const c: u32 = load16(cmd + i * 2);
            if (c == 32 || c == 9) { break; }
            emit_unit(cmd, n, &i, buf, &pos);
        }
    }
    buf[pos] = 0;
    pos = pos + 1;
    count = 1;

    // Remaining arguments.
    while (true) {
        while (i < n) {
            const c: u32 = load16(cmd + i * 2);
            if (c != 32 && c != 9) { break; }
            i = i + 1;
        }
        if (i >= n) { break; }
        if ((count as u64) + 1 >= slots_cap) { break; }

        vec[count] = ((buf as u64) + pos) as i8*;
        mut in_quotes: boolean = false;
        while (i < n) {
            const c: u32 = load16(cmd + i * 2);
            if (!in_quotes && (c == 32 || c == 9)) { break; }
            if (c == 92) {
                mut nslash: u64 = 0;
                while (i < n && load16(cmd + i * 2) == 92) {
                    nslash = nslash + 1;
                    i = i + 1;
                }
                if (i < n && load16(cmd + i * 2) == 34) {
                    mut k: u64 = 0;
                    while (k < nslash / 2) {
                        buf[pos] = 92;
                        pos = pos + 1;
                        k = k + 1;
                    }
                    if (nslash % 2 == 1) {
                        buf[pos] = 34;
                        pos = pos + 1;
                        i = i + 1;
                    } else {
                        if (in_quotes && i + 1 < n && load16(cmd + (i + 1) * 2) == 34) {
                            buf[pos] = 34;
                            pos = pos + 1;
                            i = i + 2;
                        } else {
                            in_quotes = !in_quotes;
                            i = i + 1;
                        }
                    }
                } else {
                    mut k: u64 = 0;
                    while (k < nslash) {
                        buf[pos] = 92;
                        pos = pos + 1;
                        k = k + 1;
                    }
                }
            } else if (c == 34) {
                if (in_quotes && i + 1 < n && load16(cmd + (i + 1) * 2) == 34) {
                    buf[pos] = 34;
                    pos = pos + 1;
                    i = i + 2;
                } else {
                    in_quotes = !in_quotes;
                    i = i + 1;
                }
            } else {
                emit_unit(cmd, n, &i, buf, &pos);
            }
        }
        buf[pos] = 0;
        pos = pos + 1;
        count = count + 1;
    }

    g_argc = count;
    g_argv = vec;
}

// --- Shared -----------------------------------------------------------------

/// Captured process arguments, filled by the entry before `main` runs, or left
/// 0 / null when the OS could not supply them. A freestanding program reads them
/// through `__cryo_argc` / `__cryo_argv`; a hosted one gets them as `main`'s own
/// arguments.
mut g_argc: i64  = 0;
mut g_argv: i8** = null;

/// Record the arguments the kernel already placed on the initial stack, which
/// the entry stub staged into the SysV arg registers. Nothing to decode: the
/// vector is already a NUL-terminated byte-string array, which is exactly what
/// `__cryo_argv` returns.
![config(linux)]
function capture_os_args(argc: i64, argv: i8**) -> void {
    g_argc = argc;
    g_argv = argv;
}

/// Number of process arguments (argv[0] included), or 0 when unavailable.
![no_mangle] ![config(no_runtime)]
function __cryo_argc() -> i64 {
    return g_argc;
}

/// The argv vector (NUL-terminated, argv[0] = program name), or null.
![no_mangle] ![config(no_runtime)]
function __cryo_argv() -> i8** {
    return g_argv;
}

/// Startup trampoline: capture arguments, run the user `main`, and exit with
/// its return value. Diverges — the process is gone before it could return.
/// The parameters carry the target's own C-ABI arg registers, which is where
/// Linux's `_start` staged argc/argv; on Windows they are zero and
/// `capture_os_args` sources the arguments from the PEB instead.
![no_mangle] ![config(no_runtime)]
function __cryo_start(argc: i64, argv: i8**) -> never {
    capture_os_args(argc, argv);
    Sys::rt_exit(main());
}

// --- Shared, hosted ---------------------------------------------------------

/// Apply the image relocations the entry cannot assume are already in place.
///
/// Linux: none. ld.so fully relocates the image — and sets up the thread control
/// block and `__environ` — before it transfers control to the entry, so there is
/// nothing left to fix up here.
![config(not(no_runtime))] ![config(linux)]
function run_image_fixups() -> void {
}

/// Windows: mingw's auto-import scheme leaves references to DLL *data* pointing
/// at their import thunks until `_pei386_runtime_relocator` rewrites them in
/// place. It runs before anything else because any later step may read such a
/// symbol — msvcrt's `_environ` is one, and reads as garbage without it. Only
/// data is affected; the loader binds function imports through the IAT.
![config(not(no_runtime))] ![config(windows)]
function run_image_fixups() -> void {
    _pei386_runtime_relocator();
}

/// The environment vector `main` receives as its third argument.
///
/// Linux: the kernel lays the initial stack out as `argc`, `argv[0..argc]`,
/// a NULL, then `envp[0..]`, so the environment begins one slot past argv's
/// terminator.
![config(not(no_runtime))] ![config(linux)]
function capture_os_envp(argc: i64, argv: i8**) -> i8** {
    if (argv == null) { return null; }
    return &argv[argc + 1];
}

/// Is the environment entry starting at UTF-16 unit `at` one of Windows'
/// hidden per-drive working directories?
///
/// The raw block begins with entries of the form `=C:=C:\some\path` — one per
/// drive the process has a current directory on. They are not environment
/// variables: every CRT filters them out of `environ` and `getenv`, so a program
/// has never seen them, and `std::env::vars()` splits on the first `=` and would
/// yield an EMPTY key for each. Reading the block raw is what exposes them, so
/// the filtering the CRT used to do has to happen here instead.
///
/// A leading `=` is the whole test, which is also how Windows itself marks them.
![config(not(no_runtime))] ![config(windows)]
function env_entry_is_hidden(env: u64, at: u64) -> boolean {
    return load16(env + at * 2) == 61;      // '='
}

/// Windows: from the PEB's `ProcessParameters.Environment`, transcoded the same
/// way the command line is.
///
/// Taking it from the CRT instead would mean binding to `_environ` or
/// `__p__environ`, and NEITHER is portable: `__p__environ` ships only in UCRT
/// and the MSVC-runtime import libraries, `_environ` only in msvcrt, and which
/// one a given mingw-w64 provides varies by build. Binding to either links on
/// one toolchain and fails on the next — the native build stayed green while the
/// cross-compile failed on an undefined `__p__environ`. The PEB is the same on
/// every CRT flavour and needs no import at all, which is why argv is read from
/// it too.
///
/// The block is a run of UTF-16 `KEY=VALUE` strings, each NUL-terminated, the
/// whole run closed by a second NUL — so its extent is found by scanning, not
/// from a length field. Sizing follows `capture_os_args`: at most 3 UTF-8 bytes
/// per UTF-16 unit (reaching the 4-byte form costs two units), plus the NUL each
/// entry already contributes, and one vector slot per entry plus the terminator.
///
/// Entries the CRT hides are dropped here — see `env_entry_is_hidden`.
///
/// The parameters are the shared caller's; this OS takes nothing from the stack.
/// A failure to reach the PEB, or a heap allocation failure, yields null — the
/// same "unavailable" state a program handles when the environment is empty.
![config(not(no_runtime))] ![config(windows)]
function capture_os_envp(argc: i64, argv: i8**) -> i8** {
    const peb: u64 = current_peb();
    if (peb == 0) { return null; }
    const params: u64 = load64(peb + 0x20);
    if (params == 0) { return null; }
    const env: u64 = load64(params + 0x80);
    if (env == 0) { return null; }

    // Measure: count UTF-16 units up to the closing double NUL, and the entries
    // worth keeping. `units` counts every unit, including those of skipped
    // entries — it only bounds the output buffer, so over-counting is harmless.
    // An empty block (immediate NUL) has no entries.
    mut units: u64 = 0;
    mut entries: u64 = 0;
    while (true) {
        if (load16(env + units * 2) == 0) { break; }
        const keep: boolean = !env_entry_is_hidden(env, units);
        while (load16(env + units * 2) != 0) { units = units + 1; }
        units = units + 1;          // the entry's own NUL
        if (keep) { entries = entries + 1; }
    }
    if (entries == 0) { return null; }

    const heap: u64 = load64(peb + 0x30);
    if (heap == 0) { return null; }
    const buf: u8* = RtlAllocateHeap(heap, 0, units * 3 + 1);
    if (buf == null) { return null; }
    const slots: u8* = RtlAllocateHeap(heap, 0, (entries + 1) * 8);
    if (slots == null) { return null; }
    const vec: i8** = slots as i8**;

    // Transcode. Driven by the block terminator rather than by `entries`, and
    // applying the SAME skip predicate as the measure pass, so the two cannot
    // disagree about which entry is which.
    mut pos: u64 = 0;
    mut i:   u64 = 0;
    mut n:   i64 = 0;
    while (load16(env + i * 2) != 0) {
        if (env_entry_is_hidden(env, i)) {
            while (load16(env + i * 2) != 0) { i = i + 1; }
            i = i + 1;              // step over the entry's NUL
            continue;
        }
        vec[n] = ((buf as u64) + pos) as i8*;
        while (load16(env + i * 2) != 0) {
            emit_unit(env, units, &i, buf, &pos);
        }
        i = i + 1;                  // step over the entry's NUL
        buf[pos] = 0;
        pos = pos + 1;
        n = n + 1;
    }
    vec[n] = null;                  // the vector's own terminator
    return vec;
}

/// Run the program's static constructors — the work crt0 would have driven.
///
/// Linux: `.preinit_array`, then the legacy `_init` hook, then `.init_array`,
/// each entry receiving the process arguments. That is exactly glibc's
/// `call_init` order. Skipping it leaves every static constructor unrun,
/// including crtbegin.o's `frame_dummy`, and a statically linked C++ library's
/// initializers with it.
![config(not(no_runtime))] ![config(linux)]
function run_static_ctors(argc: i32, argv: i8**, envp: i8**) -> void {
    run_init_range(preinit_array_start(), preinit_array_end(), argc, argv, envp);
    _init();
    run_init_range(init_array_start(), init_array_end(), argc, argv, envp);
}

/// Windows: mingw collects constructors behind `__main`, which takes no
/// arguments and guards against running twice.
![config(not(no_runtime))] ![config(windows)]
function run_static_ctors(argc: i32, argv: i8**, envp: i8**) -> void {
    __main();
}

/// Hosted startup: fix up the image, capture the process arguments, run the
/// static constructors, then hand off to `main` and terminate with its result.
/// Diverges — the process is gone before it could return.
///
/// The order is load bearing. Constructors are handed `(argc, argv, envp)`, so
/// the arguments must be captured before they run; `main` must come after them
/// or it observes a half-initialised program.
///
/// Termination goes through `Sys::rt_exit`, which in this tier is libc `exit`:
/// that is what flushes the program's buffered stdio and runs its `atexit`
/// handlers. Returning to the caller is not an option — nothing called us.
///
/// The parameters carry the target's own C-ABI arg registers, which is where
/// Linux's entry stub staged argc/argv; on Windows they are zero and
/// `capture_os_args` sources the arguments from the PEB instead.
![no_mangle] ![config(not(no_runtime))]
function __cryo_hosted_start(argc: i64, argv: i8**) -> never {
    run_image_fixups();
    capture_os_args(argc, argv);
    const envp: i8** = capture_os_envp(g_argc, g_argv);
    run_static_ctors(g_argc as i32, g_argv, envp);
    Sys::rt_exit(main(g_argc as i32, g_argv, envp));
}
